WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.348 --> 00:00:02.580
- Whether it's Microsoft
or Google, I mean,

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:05.259
so many people that have really hit big

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:06.609
have gone against the tide.

00:00:06.609 --> 00:00:08.426
It may seem like you have no chance,

00:00:08.426 --> 00:00:10.458
it may seem like it's over;

00:00:10.458 --> 00:00:12.979
never, ever quit.
If you're a quitter,

00:00:12.979 --> 00:00:16.255
I hope you quit right now,
and stop watching immediately.

00:00:16.255 --> 00:00:20.758
And people that can handle pressure

00:00:20.758 --> 00:00:23.313
can be entrepreneurs, can be successful.

00:00:23.313 --> 00:00:25.582
- He's an American
businessman, politician,

00:00:25.582 --> 00:00:28.543
and 45th president of the
United States of America.

00:00:28.543 --> 00:00:30.740
As of 2017, Forbes listed him

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:33.916
as the 544th wealthiest
person in the world,

00:00:33.916 --> 00:00:37.116
with a net worth of $3.5 billion.

00:00:37.116 --> 00:00:39.061
His presidential campaign received

00:00:39.061 --> 00:00:42.435
unprecedented media coverage,
and international attention.

00:00:42.435 --> 00:00:44.228
He's Donald Trump, and here's my take

00:00:44.228 --> 00:00:47.020
on his top-10 rules for
success, volume two.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.938
Rule number two is my
personal favorite, and

00:00:48.938 --> 00:00:51.364
I'm curious to figure out which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:51.364 --> 00:00:53.214
Also, guys, as you're
watching, if you hear something

00:00:53.214 --> 00:00:55.215
that really resonates
with you, please leave it

00:00:55.215 --> 00:00:56.965
down in the comments
below, put quotes around it

00:00:56.965 --> 00:00:59.631
so other people can be
inspired by your sharing.

00:00:59.631 --> 00:01:01.027
And also, when you write it down,

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:04.065
you're much more likely to
lock it in for yourself too.

00:01:04.065 --> 00:01:04.898
Enjoy.

00:01:08.417 --> 00:01:12.084
(inspirational theme music)

00:01:17.951 --> 00:01:19.831
- What would be one word
of advice you would have

00:01:19.831 --> 00:01:23.998
for an upcoming businessman,
or for a future businessman?

00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:28.454
Or woman, or woman.
- Knowledge.

00:01:28.454 --> 00:01:30.431
Knowledge, but, if it's one word.

00:01:30.431 --> 00:01:33.283
One word's very hard for that,
but the word is knowledge,

00:01:33.283 --> 00:01:36.569
but you get knowledge through experience.

00:01:36.569 --> 00:01:38.830
One of the things people
talk about is luck,

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:40.682
and I do believe that people are lucky,

00:01:40.682 --> 00:01:42.303
and some people are luckier than others.

00:01:42.303 --> 00:01:43.696
I do believe that.

00:01:43.696 --> 00:01:45.961
But there's also an expression:
the harder you work,

00:01:45.961 --> 00:01:47.388
the luckier you get.

00:01:47.388 --> 00:01:49.011
And I can tell you 10 instances

00:01:49.011 --> 00:01:52.905
where I didn't quit; one, it was 1990,

00:01:52.905 --> 00:01:56.423
the world was coming down, the
debt markets were everybody.

00:01:56.423 --> 00:01:58.795
All of my friends and
enemies were going bankrupt;

00:01:58.795 --> 00:02:00.042
I never did.

00:02:00.042 --> 00:02:04.265
And, the times were
actually just terrible,

00:02:04.265 --> 00:02:06.968
and even today, you look at
times, and you see what happens,

00:02:06.968 --> 00:02:08.319
and you have to learn.

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:10.736
But, the word luck is very interesting,

00:02:10.736 --> 00:02:13.718
but if I didn't work really hard,

00:02:13.718 --> 00:02:16.880
I probably would not,
almost definitely would not

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:20.174
be here today, and unless
you had me up as a man

00:02:20.174 --> 00:02:22.809
who, at one point, was successful,

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:25.647
but let's see what a loser looks like.

00:02:25.647 --> 00:02:29.323
So, you have to work
hard, because it is true,

00:02:29.323 --> 00:02:32.559
you can develop luck, through hard work.

00:02:32.559 --> 00:02:34.988
- Your first book, "The Art of the Deal",

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:37.170
begins with a simple sentence

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.687
that many people might
find hard to understand.

00:02:39.687 --> 00:02:42.592
You say: I don't do it for the money.

00:02:42.592 --> 00:02:43.746
Can you explain that?

00:02:43.746 --> 00:02:45.248
- Well, I do it because I love it.

00:02:45.248 --> 00:02:47.016
If I didn't love it, I wouldn't do it.

00:02:47.016 --> 00:02:48.824
I have bad days, I have good days,

00:02:48.824 --> 00:02:50.280
I have days somewhere in the middle.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.022
But, the end result is:
I love what I'm doing,

00:02:53.022 --> 00:02:54.220
and I don't do it for the money.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.099
And I make money because, probably,

00:02:56.099 --> 00:02:57.353
I'm not doing it for the money.

00:02:57.353 --> 00:02:59.301
I do it, and I do it well.

00:02:59.301 --> 00:03:01.099
I'm the biggest developer in New York.

00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:02.524
I love building buildings.

00:03:02.524 --> 00:03:04.499
I have a lot of fun building buildings.

00:03:04.499 --> 00:03:07.604
The fact is, I do it for
fun, I do it for game,

00:03:07.604 --> 00:03:10.742
I do it for sport; I happen to
make a lot of money doing it.

00:03:10.742 --> 00:03:13.667
If I didn't enjoy it,
if I didn't really love

00:03:13.667 --> 00:03:16.107
what I'm doing, I wouldn't be successful,

00:03:16.107 --> 00:03:17.587
I wouldn't be sitting with you today.

00:03:17.587 --> 00:03:19.486
- [Interviewer] What's the
implication of that, for someone

00:03:19.486 --> 00:03:21.441
watching this, who wants
to create greater wealth?

00:03:21.441 --> 00:03:23.180
What's the message in that, do you think?

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.761
- Well, the message is: you
have to love what you do.

00:03:25.761 --> 00:03:27.751
If you do it for the money alone,

00:03:27.751 --> 00:03:29.552
it's probably not going to work.

00:03:29.552 --> 00:03:31.516
I have friends, where I tell them,

00:03:31.516 --> 00:03:33.535
get out of this business,
which is a good business,

00:03:33.535 --> 00:03:35.705
and go into another
business, which isn't as good

00:03:35.705 --> 00:03:38.557
a business, because they like
the other business better.

00:03:38.557 --> 00:03:42.272
They'll do better in a
less-good business, so to speak,

00:03:42.272 --> 00:03:44.926
than they will in the better
business, because they like it.

00:03:44.926 --> 00:03:47.993
Going against the tide
is great, if it works.

00:03:47.993 --> 00:03:49.472
Now, it doesn't always work,

00:03:49.472 --> 00:03:52.389
and you really have to be
smart to go against the tide,

00:03:52.389 --> 00:03:55.109
because you're going against
a lot of smart people.

00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:58.057
But when you get it right,
you'll end up better,

00:03:58.057 --> 00:04:00.091
whether it's Microsoft or Google.

00:04:00.091 --> 00:04:02.892
I mean, so many people
that have really hit big

00:04:02.892 --> 00:04:04.568
have gone against the tide.

00:04:04.568 --> 00:04:06.779
But you have to have great
confidence in yourself,

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:08.921
you have to be the right person.

00:04:08.921 --> 00:04:10.827
There are very few people
that can pull it off,

00:04:10.827 --> 00:04:13.121
but when you pull it off, it's great.

00:04:13.121 --> 00:04:15.939
Same thing in real estate;
you go against the tide,

00:04:15.939 --> 00:04:19.145
people are selling, you're
buying, the tide changes.

00:04:19.145 --> 00:04:21.609
Never, ever give up.

00:04:21.609 --> 00:04:24.718
Just keep going forward; never stop.

00:04:24.718 --> 00:04:26.995
It may seem like you have no chance,

00:04:26.995 --> 00:04:28.983
it may seem like it's over.

00:04:28.983 --> 00:04:31.554
Never, ever quit.
If you're a quitter,

00:04:31.554 --> 00:04:34.770
I hope you quit right now,
and stop watching immediately,

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.402
because you'll never make it.

00:04:36.402 --> 00:04:38.443
- [Interviewer] Now, you
own these incredible casinos

00:04:38.443 --> 00:04:41.941
and hotels, and the
towers in New York City,

00:04:41.941 --> 00:04:44.488
you're going to build this
world's tallest building now,

00:04:44.488 --> 00:04:45.828
in Television City.

00:04:45.828 --> 00:04:48.716
When is enough enough,
or will Donald Trump

00:04:48.716 --> 00:04:52.452
never be satisfied with the
deal-making and the acquisition?

00:04:52.452 --> 00:04:54.181
- Well, I'm like a lot of
my friends from Houston,

00:04:54.181 --> 00:04:55.119
I just keep chugging.

00:04:55.119 --> 00:04:56.718
I mean, all you can do is just keep going.

00:04:56.718 --> 00:04:59.969
Life is sort of a pretty short experience,

00:04:59.969 --> 00:05:03.198
and you have to maintain
some kind of an equilibrium,

00:05:03.198 --> 00:05:05.596
and the way I maintain that is to work.

00:05:05.596 --> 00:05:08.362
And I enjoy what I do, I love what I do,

00:05:08.362 --> 00:05:10.424
and I hope I do it well.

00:05:10.424 --> 00:05:12.421
And so, I just keep going forward, Dan,

00:05:12.421 --> 00:05:14.160
and it's, so far, it's paid off.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.147
- Are you smarter than most people

00:05:16.147 --> 00:05:18.251
who would call themselves entrepreneurs?

00:05:18.251 --> 00:05:19.266
Do you work harder?

00:05:19.266 --> 00:05:20.865
What is the deal with you, anyway?

00:05:20.865 --> 00:05:21.986
- Well, that's an interesting question.

00:05:21.986 --> 00:05:23.402
I don't know if anyone's
ever asked me the question,

00:05:23.402 --> 00:05:24.806
am I smarter than other people.

00:05:24.806 --> 00:05:27.602
I think that, without a
certain innate intelligence,

00:05:27.602 --> 00:05:30.518
and without a certain drive,
you're not going to be successful.

00:05:30.518 --> 00:05:34.438
I have a friend who was
not successful at all,

00:05:34.438 --> 00:05:37.504
but was really up-and-coming, and

00:05:37.504 --> 00:05:40.861
he had a thing, he would
only fly first class.

00:05:40.861 --> 00:05:43.756
I'm not saying do this, because
for somebody it won't work,

00:05:43.756 --> 00:05:45.613
but he needed that mentally.

00:05:45.613 --> 00:05:48.876
He wanted to fly first
class, because mentally,

00:05:48.876 --> 00:05:52.038
he wanted to think he was
the best, and that's it.

00:05:52.038 --> 00:05:54.400
And, even though he didn't
have much money at the time,

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.606
this is years ago, he would always fly,

00:05:56.606 --> 00:05:58.215
I used to criticize him.

00:05:58.215 --> 00:05:59.640
But it put him in a good state of mind,

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:01.776
and he became a very, very successful guy,

00:06:01.776 --> 00:06:03.417
very, very successful.

00:06:03.417 --> 00:06:05.489
And, I've always remembered that.

00:06:05.489 --> 00:06:07.101
He would never fly coach.

00:06:07.101 --> 00:06:08.494
He would always fly first class,

00:06:08.494 --> 00:06:09.808
even though he didn't
have the means to fly.

00:06:09.808 --> 00:06:13.157
So, look, it's complicated.

00:06:13.157 --> 00:06:15.792
But, whatever it takes to train that.

00:06:15.792 --> 00:06:17.889
I've always believed in positive thinking.

00:06:17.889 --> 00:06:20.737
At the same time, I don't want to mislead,

00:06:20.737 --> 00:06:23.898
I also believe in aspiring,

00:06:23.898 --> 00:06:26.057
in terms of what you're doing,

00:06:26.057 --> 00:06:29.178
aspiring to protect against the down-side.

00:06:29.178 --> 00:06:30.798
You can't just be this wonderful guy,

00:06:30.798 --> 00:06:32.923
walking around, everything's
positive, because,

00:06:32.923 --> 00:06:35.636
unfortunately, that's
not the way the world is.

00:06:35.636 --> 00:06:39.200
I love to tell people to
think about the challenges,

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:42.049
so that when they do
come along, you're ready.

00:06:42.049 --> 00:06:44.123
- Let's suppose that I have a job,

00:06:44.123 --> 00:06:47.359
but I have the feeling that I
want to start my own business.

00:06:47.359 --> 00:06:50.602
How would I know that I'm
the right kind of person

00:06:50.602 --> 00:06:52.505
for the entrepreneurial life?

00:06:52.505 --> 00:06:54.165
- To me, that's the best
question you've asked,

00:06:54.165 --> 00:06:55.513
and it's also the toughest.

00:06:55.513 --> 00:06:58.137
Somebody has a family, has a job,

00:06:58.137 --> 00:07:00.936
they have a nice job,
they get X dollars a week,

00:07:00.936 --> 00:07:02.768
they don't have to worry about very much.

00:07:02.768 --> 00:07:04.122
It's not going to be great,

00:07:04.122 --> 00:07:05.875
they're not going to live in Palm Beach,

00:07:05.875 --> 00:07:07.786
but you know what?
They have a job,

00:07:07.786 --> 00:07:09.971
and it's income coming in.

00:07:09.971 --> 00:07:13.539
The hardest thing to do,
in my opinion, in business,

00:07:13.539 --> 00:07:16.553
is to leave that job, and
become an entrepreneur,

00:07:16.553 --> 00:07:18.788
because you're risking your wealth,

00:07:18.788 --> 00:07:21.741
you're risking your family, your health,

00:07:21.741 --> 00:07:23.044
your family's health.

00:07:23.044 --> 00:07:24.827
You're really risking a lot.

00:07:24.827 --> 00:07:27.126
You have to know, in your gut,

00:07:27.126 --> 00:07:29.382
whether or not you have
it, to be an entrepreneur.

00:07:29.382 --> 00:07:30.581
Can you handle pressure?

00:07:30.581 --> 00:07:32.158
Can you handle all of the problems

00:07:32.158 --> 00:07:33.778
that you're going to be confronted with?

00:07:33.778 --> 00:07:35.492
And there'll be many, many problems.

00:07:35.492 --> 00:07:39.221
But the toughest job that
somebody has, in my opinion,

00:07:39.221 --> 00:07:42.340
in business, is to know when
to get out of something,

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:44.144
and when to go in something, because

00:07:44.144 --> 00:07:47.157
that's a very, very risky proposition.

00:07:47.157 --> 00:07:49.715
You know, when people
ask me about success,

00:07:49.715 --> 00:07:52.199
I just started thinking about
it over the last couple years,

00:07:52.199 --> 00:07:54.573
'cause I've seen a lot of it.

00:07:54.573 --> 00:07:58.820
You have to have an
ability to handle pressure,

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:01.745
because you, no matter
how successful you are,

00:08:01.745 --> 00:08:03.828
I have many, many friends, and enemies,

00:08:03.828 --> 00:08:06.259
lot of enemies too, I don't
care, but they're smart.

00:08:06.259 --> 00:08:07.887
(audience laughing)
I have a lot of enemies.

00:08:07.887 --> 00:08:11.605
And, I've watched
people, and I've seen it,

00:08:11.605 --> 00:08:16.267
and people that can handle pressure

00:08:16.267 --> 00:08:18.414
can be entrepreneurs, can be successful.

00:08:18.414 --> 00:08:22.464
Now, I have some friends that
are really, really smart,

00:08:22.464 --> 00:08:24.156
but they can't handle pressure.

00:08:24.156 --> 00:08:25.962
In which case, they
should work for somebody,

00:08:25.962 --> 00:08:27.291
do great, and have a good life.

00:08:27.291 --> 00:08:28.997
There's nothing wrong with it,

00:08:28.997 --> 00:08:31.059
'cause I almost think
that's an instinctive thing,

00:08:31.059 --> 00:08:32.270
the ability to handle pressure.

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:35.736
Now, one of the things I
tell people about pressure

00:08:35.736 --> 00:08:37.587
is, you know what they said?

00:08:37.587 --> 00:08:38.553
How do you handle pressure?

00:08:38.553 --> 00:08:39.936
Who's had more pressure than me?

00:08:39.936 --> 00:08:41.144
Ai-ai-ai.

00:08:41.144 --> 00:08:43.705
Have I had pressure over the years!

00:08:43.705 --> 00:08:46.133
And, you know, I mean, I
get criticized at my hair,

00:08:46.133 --> 00:08:48.961
but it's not so bad, and
it's real, and it's mine.

00:08:48.961 --> 00:08:52.194
You know it is my real hair.
(audience applauding)

00:08:52.194 --> 00:08:54.134
I mean, I get killed.

00:08:54.134 --> 00:08:57.082
I had an article recently,
that was so good,

00:08:57.082 --> 00:08:59.620
it was such a great article,

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:02.158
and then it had one line, but
he wears the worst hairpiece

00:09:02.158 --> 00:09:04.308
I've ever seen; I couldn't
show the article to anybody,

00:09:04.308 --> 00:09:06.327
and it's not even true.

00:09:06.327 --> 00:09:10.191
But you have to have the
ability to handle pressure.

00:09:10.191 --> 00:09:12.216
And if you can't handle pressure,

00:09:12.216 --> 00:09:16.502
you have to know that about yourself.

00:09:16.502 --> 00:09:19.557
You have to know that about yourself.

00:09:19.557 --> 00:09:23.033
So, handle pressure.

00:09:23.033 --> 00:09:25.368
If you can't, that's okay.

00:09:25.368 --> 00:09:28.197
But one of the things I tell
people about handling pressure

00:09:28.197 --> 00:09:30.615
is, remember, 'cause
they ask me the question:

00:09:30.615 --> 00:09:33.068
how do you handle the stress?

00:09:33.068 --> 00:09:35.011
And you know, somebody actually said:

00:09:35.011 --> 00:09:37.048
Donald, you're so handsome.

00:09:37.048 --> 00:09:39.208
Do you believe this?
I love this person.

00:09:39.208 --> 00:09:40.504
(audience laughing)

00:09:40.504 --> 00:09:44.212
You're so handsome; how
have you stayed so handsome,

00:09:44.212 --> 00:09:46.641
under all this pressure?

00:09:46.641 --> 00:09:48.819
And I actually thought of it, to myself,

00:09:48.819 --> 00:09:51.607
you have to remember, it doesn't matter.

00:09:51.607 --> 00:09:54.340
God matters, your family matters.

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:56.542
It doesn't matter, and if you say it,

00:09:56.542 --> 00:09:59.781
I have some people, they say:
I've got to be successful,

00:09:59.781 --> 00:10:00.849
I've got to be.

00:10:00.849 --> 00:10:03.410
They'll never be
successful; it's too crazy.

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:05.261
They can't think straight.

00:10:05.261 --> 00:10:06.094
Does that make sense?

00:10:06.094 --> 00:10:08.234
Does that make sense to
you, that was going crazy,

00:10:08.234 --> 00:10:10.990
standing up about your success, right?

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:13.662
So, you've got to handle--

00:10:13.662 --> 00:10:15.564
look, now he's a little bit more low-key.

00:10:15.564 --> 00:10:17.174
That's very good, I like that.

00:10:17.174 --> 00:10:19.223
We've calmed him down.

00:10:19.223 --> 00:10:21.566
So, you've got to be able,

00:10:21.566 --> 00:10:25.054
you've got to be able to
sort of say to yourself,

00:10:25.054 --> 00:10:27.769
nothing matters that much,

00:10:27.769 --> 00:10:30.472
other than the real big deals,

00:10:30.472 --> 00:10:33.248
with family, with your faith, et. cetera.

00:10:33.248 --> 00:10:37.393
People settle for mediocrity,
for one reason: they're lazy.

00:10:37.393 --> 00:10:38.976
I've seen it so often;

00:10:38.976 --> 00:10:41.245
people go into something,
they don't want to go

00:10:41.245 --> 00:10:44.076
that extra step, they know
it's not going to be great,

00:10:44.076 --> 00:10:45.725
it might be good or it might be okay,

00:10:45.725 --> 00:10:47.435
it's not going to be great.

00:10:47.435 --> 00:10:50.142
They settle for mediocrity; they're lazy.

00:10:50.142 --> 00:10:51.471
I have five children, but the three

00:10:51.471 --> 00:10:53.424
that are in business, and
a lot of it is because

00:10:53.424 --> 00:10:55.325
of the success of the
Apprentice, where they go on,

00:10:55.325 --> 00:10:58.490
and people get to know
Ivanka, and Don, and Eric.

00:10:58.490 --> 00:11:00.976
But, a lot of that was, early upbringing,

00:11:00.976 --> 00:11:03.759
I'd always say to 'em, no drugs,

00:11:03.759 --> 00:11:05.636
no alcohol, no cigarettes,

00:11:05.636 --> 00:11:08.122
and now I add, for Barron,
and I drive him crazy,

00:11:08.122 --> 00:11:10.530
I'd say, every time
they'd leave, no drugs,

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:13.146
no alcohol, no cigarettes,
repeat after me.

00:11:13.146 --> 00:11:15.130
And I'd have to, they'd
go, Ivanka would go,

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:16.772
dad, will you leave me alone.

00:11:16.772 --> 00:11:18.530
But I'd drum it into their heads,

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:20.680
and you know, you have to get through

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:22.476
those artificial barriers.

00:11:22.476 --> 00:11:23.727
I call them artificial barriers,

00:11:23.727 --> 00:11:25.443
because you can do something about it,

00:11:25.443 --> 00:11:26.759
until it's too late.

00:11:26.759 --> 00:11:29.680
Once it's in your system,
it's very hard to stop.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:33.003
Like, I don't drink, and it's
very easy for me not to drink.

00:11:33.003 --> 00:11:34.827
I tell people, what are you drinking for?

00:11:34.827 --> 00:11:36.338
What are you drinking for?

00:11:36.338 --> 00:11:37.785
And they don't even
understand what I'm saying,

00:11:37.785 --> 00:11:38.628
because it's so--

00:11:38.628 --> 00:11:40.418
- How did you, it's amazing

00:11:40.418 --> 00:11:43.055
when people see you, they can't imagine,

00:11:43.055 --> 00:11:44.009
no alcohol.

00:11:44.009 --> 00:11:46.970
- Oh, would I be in
trouble if I was a drinker,

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:49.183
with my life.
- No smoking.

00:11:49.183 --> 00:11:50.728
- Yeah, no smoking.

00:11:50.728 --> 00:11:52.055
Well, I had a brother--
- And, no drugs.

00:11:52.055 --> 00:11:56.330
- I had a brother, Fred, who
was, sadly, an alcoholic,

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:59.616
a serious alcoholic, and
he was the best-looking guy

00:11:59.616 --> 00:12:01.112
you've ever seen.
- Yeah, he was much older

00:12:01.112 --> 00:12:02.039
than you.
- He was much older.

00:12:02.039 --> 00:12:03.789
He was 10 years older than
me, 11 years older than me,

00:12:03.789 --> 00:12:06.985
and he was as good-looking
a guy as you've ever seen.

00:12:06.985 --> 00:12:10.618
He had everything going;
the best personality.

00:12:10.618 --> 00:12:12.853
I have like a bad
personality compared to Fred,

00:12:12.853 --> 00:12:17.019
but he started drinking,
and he also smoked a lot,

00:12:17.019 --> 00:12:19.019
but he started drinking.

00:12:20.290 --> 00:12:22.808
I think it started in college, Frankly,

00:12:22.808 --> 00:12:25.094
but it got progressively worse.

00:12:25.094 --> 00:12:28.973
And he was really unable to
stop, and he'd always tell me,

00:12:28.973 --> 00:12:30.352
don't you ever drink.

00:12:30.352 --> 00:12:31.739
So, he was much older than me,

00:12:31.739 --> 00:12:33.002
and it really has an impact, you know,

00:12:33.002 --> 00:12:35.126
when you have that kind
of an age difference.

00:12:35.126 --> 00:12:37.722
But he'd point, don't you ever drink,

00:12:37.722 --> 00:12:39.813
and don't you ever smoke,
he'd add the smoking,

00:12:39.813 --> 00:12:42.071
'cause he smoked and he drank.

00:12:42.071 --> 00:12:45.809
And it hugely affected his
health, and hugely affected,

00:12:45.809 --> 00:12:49.597
even his look, and his, you
know, the whole bearing.

00:12:49.597 --> 00:12:51.271
And every time he'd see me, he's say:

00:12:51.271 --> 00:12:52.945
don't you ever drink, and don't you--

00:12:52.945 --> 00:12:55.163
so, I never drank.

00:12:55.163 --> 00:12:56.836
I mean, I really, when
you talk about my father,

00:12:56.836 --> 00:12:57.764
I learned a lot from my father,

00:12:57.764 --> 00:13:00.238
but I learned a lot from my brother Fred.

00:13:00.238 --> 00:13:03.015
And he set an example, it
wasn't maybe the example

00:13:03.015 --> 00:13:04.696
the people would think, but it really was,

00:13:04.696 --> 00:13:06.395
in its own way, an example.

00:13:06.395 --> 00:13:10.033
- We met an adorable
10-year-old girl, named Megan.

00:13:10.033 --> 00:13:12.775
Now, Megan was born with
brittle bone disease.

00:13:12.775 --> 00:13:14.837
- Hi Megan, my name's Donald,

00:13:14.837 --> 00:13:17.265
and you probably don't know me but

00:13:17.265 --> 00:13:19.588
I was watching Maury's show the other day,

00:13:19.588 --> 00:13:23.086
and I must tell you, you
really hit me right here.

00:13:23.086 --> 00:13:25.366
I think you are so beautiful,

00:13:25.366 --> 00:13:27.267
and, both inside and out.

00:13:27.267 --> 00:13:29.403
I had a little something, a little gift,

00:13:29.403 --> 00:13:31.866
that I have to Maury,
who's a friend of mine,

00:13:31.866 --> 00:13:34.305
and a very good golfer;
don't ever play him in golf.

00:13:34.305 --> 00:13:36.476
He's very, very good, believe me.

00:13:36.476 --> 00:13:38.344
And, I gave him a little gift for you,

00:13:38.344 --> 00:13:41.474
and I hope you and your mother
have a good time with it,

00:13:41.474 --> 00:13:43.795
and you're very special,
and you just keep it up,

00:13:43.795 --> 00:13:45.246
and keep up that attitude.

00:13:45.246 --> 00:13:47.986
So, good luck, and you stay in touch.

00:13:47.986 --> 00:13:50.115
(audience applauding)

00:13:50.115 --> 00:13:52.530
- So--
- Unbelievable!

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:53.780
- Donald Trump,

00:13:55.222 --> 00:13:58.696
Donald Trump, he has his
name on more buildings

00:13:58.696 --> 00:14:01.158
in New York City, than any other person.

00:14:01.158 --> 00:14:02.820
And, besides that,

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:06.342
he is one of the most
generous people I know.

00:14:06.342 --> 00:14:09.569
And he wants you and your mom

00:14:09.569 --> 00:14:12.624
to have a very special check.

00:14:12.624 --> 00:14:14.922
(audience applauding)

00:14:14.922 --> 00:14:17.872
And, when we talk about Donald Trump,

00:14:17.872 --> 00:14:19.319
when he gives out checks,

00:14:19.319 --> 00:14:21.929
we're not talking chump change here.

00:14:21.929 --> 00:14:23.368
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:14:23.368 --> 00:14:25.627
This is big-time money.

00:14:25.627 --> 00:14:27.044
So, look at that.

00:14:28.432 --> 00:14:30.743
Do you know how much that's for?

00:14:30.743 --> 00:14:33.660
Did you get all those zeroes right?

00:14:35.817 --> 00:14:37.973
(audience applauding)

00:14:37.973 --> 00:14:39.845
- Hi, Donald.

00:14:39.845 --> 00:14:41.031
Good.

00:14:41.031 --> 00:14:44.284
I really wanted to say
thank you very much.

00:14:44.284 --> 00:14:45.148
Bye.

00:14:45.148 --> 00:14:47.339
I told him that I thanked him,

00:14:47.339 --> 00:14:49.315
and he said, well you're very welcome,

00:14:49.315 --> 00:14:52.648
and he told me to keep up the good work.

00:14:53.698 --> 00:14:55.208
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:14:55.208 --> 00:14:57.108
I'd love to know, what did
you think of this video?

00:14:57.108 --> 00:14:59.561
And in general, what do you
think of the volume two series?

00:14:59.561 --> 00:15:01.126
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:15:01.126 --> 00:15:03.568
Also, I'd love to know, what
did you learn from this video,

00:15:03.568 --> 00:15:05.370
that had the biggest impact on you?

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:07.841
What did he say that
resonated with you the most,

00:15:07.841 --> 00:15:09.764
and you're going to immediately apply,

00:15:09.764 --> 00:15:11.967
somehow, to your life,
or to your business?

00:15:11.967 --> 00:15:13.447
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:15:13.447 --> 00:15:14.665
I'm super-curious to find out.

00:15:14.665 --> 00:15:15.930
Finally, I wanted to
give a quick shout-out

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:16.988
to Kelly Quittenton.

00:15:16.988 --> 00:15:19.310
Kelly, thank you so much
for picking up ten copies

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:22.054
of my book, and sharing
it with your friends.

00:15:22.054 --> 00:15:24.085
I really, really, really
appreciate the support,

00:15:24.085 --> 00:15:26.066
and I hope you all enjoy the read.

00:15:26.066 --> 00:15:27.453
So, thank you guys again for watching.

00:15:27.453 --> 00:15:30.713
I believe in you; I hope you
continue to believe in yourself

00:15:30.713 --> 00:15:32.624
and whatever your one word is.

00:15:32.624 --> 00:15:35.041
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:15:41.252 --> 00:15:44.199
- You have an enormous
skill with branding,

00:15:44.199 --> 00:15:46.782
and marketing, and re-branding.

00:15:48.049 --> 00:15:50.051
What would you give as advice

00:15:50.051 --> 00:15:53.010
to small and medium-sized business owners,

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:55.420
for branding and marketing?
- Well, the first thing

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:56.846
is you have to have a good product.

00:15:56.846 --> 00:15:58.358
If you don't have the product, forget it.

00:15:58.358 --> 00:15:59.848
But assuming they have
a really good product,

00:15:59.848 --> 00:16:00.789
you have to get the word out.

00:16:00.789 --> 00:16:04.221
You know, I know people
that are better singers,

00:16:04.221 --> 00:16:06.306
more talented, than Frank Sinatra.

00:16:06.306 --> 00:16:08.033
You'll never hear about 'em.

00:16:08.033 --> 00:16:11.918
I know one in particular, a
person who really is talented,

00:16:11.918 --> 00:16:14.738
always practicing, always rehearsing,

00:16:14.738 --> 00:16:16.761
and yet, if the New York
Times calls for an interview,

00:16:16.761 --> 00:16:18.654
no, no, I'm not ready
yet, I'm not ready yet.

00:16:18.654 --> 00:16:19.983
They're never ready.

00:16:19.983 --> 00:16:21.301
You got to get out and do it.

00:16:21.301 --> 00:16:23.547
Also, you have to sort
of let the press know

00:16:23.547 --> 00:16:27.156
that you exist, getting good
ratings on things, like,

00:16:27.156 --> 00:16:29.235
I have golf courses
that have high ratings.

00:16:29.235 --> 00:16:30.507
And then, once you get the ratings,

00:16:30.507 --> 00:16:32.962
you got to let people
know you have the ratings.

00:16:32.962 --> 00:16:36.231
It's a long process, and
it takes a long time.

00:16:36.231 --> 00:16:38.189
It's really just a wonderful thing,

00:16:38.189 --> 00:16:39.636
if you can have a mentor.

00:16:39.636 --> 00:16:42.182
Having a mentor makes a
big difference in life.

00:16:42.182 --> 00:16:43.931
Now, you have to look around;

00:16:43.931 --> 00:16:47.595
who is somebody that you
aspire to be, or to be like?

00:16:47.595 --> 00:16:49.850
But having a mentor is a wonderful thing.

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:51.455
- Something I do have to ask you is,

00:16:51.455 --> 00:16:54.167
what's it like to wake
up and be Donald Trump?

00:16:54.167 --> 00:16:55.981
When you're shaving in the mirror,

00:16:55.981 --> 00:16:58.803
and you're looking at
yourself, you're an icon.

00:16:58.803 --> 00:17:00.088
What is that like?

00:17:00.088 --> 00:17:01.631
- Well, I'm honored by your question,

00:17:01.631 --> 00:17:03.111
but I don't really
think about it that way.

00:17:03.111 --> 00:17:04.859
I mean, I wake up and I do shave,

00:17:04.859 --> 00:17:07.622
and I do use a mirror, and
all of that stuff, but,

00:17:07.622 --> 00:17:10.828
you know, I go to work,
I'm in my office early,

00:17:10.828 --> 00:17:13.325
I leave late, I work hard,
I employ a lot of people,

00:17:13.325 --> 00:17:16.197
lots of education, lots of everything

00:17:16.197 --> 00:17:19.910
I supply for people, by giving
them a check every week.

00:17:19.910 --> 00:17:22.515
Lots of healthcare, frankly,
and it makes me feel good.

00:17:22.515 --> 00:17:25.200
I know a lot of people where,
couple of my employees,

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:26.639
their wives have cancer,

00:17:26.639 --> 00:17:27.987
and they're being well taken care of,

00:17:27.987 --> 00:17:29.788
because they have a good program,

00:17:29.788 --> 00:17:33.191
under the Trump organization,
and I'm happy about that.

00:17:33.191 --> 00:17:36.465
So, I just feel I've
done a really good job.

00:17:36.465 --> 00:17:37.827
I've created a lot of quality,

00:17:37.827 --> 00:17:40.827
and I've given a lot of people jobs.

